  "knowledgePattern": "Carry the exponential range through a monotone outer function",
  "hints": [
    {
      "title": "Find the attainable inner range",
      "body": "<p>Name the exponential as a positive variable. Use the full sine range, remembering that a base below one reverses exponent order.</p>",
      "recap": "An exponential with a trigonometric exponent has endpoint values given by the attained extreme exponents; the order depends on the base.",
      "pitfall": "The base-one case is constant and should not disappear from the analysis.",
      "pause": "Find the inner range for each possible base regime.",
      "recall": [
        {
          "lessonId": "p1-b2-l05",
          "reminder": "An exponential with a trigonometric exponent has endpoint values given by the attained extreme exponents; the order depends on the base."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Carry that range through the outer expression",
      "body": "<p>Compare the outer expression at two ordered positive inputs to decide where its extreme values lie.</p>",
      "recap": "For two positive inputs, taking reciprocals reverses their order; track how each term of the outer expression changes.",
      "pitfall": "You need both attained extremes, not separate guesses for the two terms at incompatible inputs.",
      "pause": "Form the product of the actual extrema, then solve its strict sign condition.",
      "recall": [
        {
          "lessonId": "p1-b1-l07",
          "reminder": "For two positive inputs, taking reciprocals reverses their order; track how each term of the outer expression changes."
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
  "solution": "<p><strong>1.</strong> Put <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>sin</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>x</mi></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>. Since sine attains every value in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math>, the two attained endpoints of u are t and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></math>, with their order reversed when <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>. For <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> both are 1.</p><p><strong>2.</strong> The outer expression is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow></math>, which strictly increases for positive u: for <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>, its increase is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>. Thus the two extrema of y are <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>t</mi></mrow></math>, in some order.</p><p><strong>3.</strong> Their product is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. Multiplying its strict positivity condition by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> gives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn><msup><mi>t</mi><mn>4</mn></msup><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>5</mn><msup><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>, or <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>2</mn><msup><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>.</p><p><strong>4.</strong> The quadratic sign interval is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>. As <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>, this is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt></mrow></math>, answer C. At the two endpoints one extreme is zero and the product fails to be positive; at t=1 the constant y=−1 gives positive product 1, so it is correctly included.</p>"
}
